/// \defgroup grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories AI update and special-ability update modules and their factories
/// \ingroup grp_objects_modules
/// \brief Fifteen of BFME 2's update module classes, eight whose names
///        end in AIUpdate and seven special-ability updates, with the
///        instance factories that create them and four of their module
///        data classes with those classes' data factories.
///
/// Each class here is a module that an `Object` block in INI attaches with
/// a `Behavior = <ModuleName> ModuleTag_...` line. This group documents how
/// the module and its data come into existence and which INI fields the
/// data accepts. It does not describe what the modules do every frame or
/// how AIUpdateInterface and SpecialAbilityUpdate work inside; the class
/// pages stay at the level of role, base class and INI contract. BFME 2
/// registers many other AI and special-ability update modules (for
/// example AssaultTransportAIUpdate, DozerAIUpdate and WorkerAIUpdate);
/// only the classes listed below are covered here.
///
/// \par Two factories per module name
/// ModuleFactory::init registers each module name with an instance factory,
/// a data factory, a module type and an interface mask; the registrations
/// are listed in `reverse/module_factory_registrations.csv`. All fifteen
/// names are spelled exactly like their classes and are registered as
/// logic (behavior) modules.
/// - The **data factory** runs while INI loads.
///   ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it with the INI reader for
///   each module line. It creates the module data with its constructor
///   defaults and, when the reader is not null, parses the module's block
///   into it with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and the data class's static
///   \c buildFieldParse.
/// - The **instance factory** runs when an object is built.
///   ModuleFactory::newModule calls it with the new object and the module
///   data parsed earlier, and it creates the module.
///
/// \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories walks through this flow
/// step by step; it applies here unchanged. Neither factory is called
/// directly: the module factory reaches them only through the registered
/// pointers, so no direct caller is known.
///
/// \par Shared data factories
/// A data factory need not be unique to its module name. The registrations
/// show two cases in this group:
/// - "AIUpdateInterface" and "TransportAIUpdate" are registered with the
///   same data factory, TransportAIUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData.
///   Zero Hour does the same: there TransportAIUpdate has no data class of
///   its own and inherits AIUpdateInterface's data factory.
/// - "HordeAIUpdate" and "HordeWorkerAIUpdate" share one data factory,
///   HordeAIUpdate::friend_newModuleData. It is not documented here; its
///   retail body builds data with the shared AI constructor and parses it
///   with the shared AI field table described below.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// - One module data object exists per module line per object template. It
///   is shared, read-only in practice, by every object built from that
///   template, and the module factory keeps it in its own list.
/// - One module exists per object whose template lists it, owned by that
///   object. Zero Hour: the object's destructor deletes its modules and the
///   module factory's destructor deletes the module data.
///
/// \par Implementation notes common to the factories
/// - Every instance factory allocates the module with the global
///   \c operator \c new (\ref sub_memory) at the class's size, runs the
///   class's constructor with the object and the module data, and returns
///   the module as the Module base the factory stores. An exception frame
///   around the constructor frees the block if the constructor throws, as
///   a C++ new-expression requires; when the allocation returns null the
///   constructor is skipped and null is returned.
/// - Every data factory documented here has the same frame and then calls
///   INI::initFromINIMultiProc after construction, outside the exception
///   frame, so an INI error thrown while parsing does not free the new data.
/// - Zero Hour generates both functions inline from the module macros in
///   `Common/Module.h`; there the module comes from the class's memory pool
///   and the data from a tagged \c new. The BFME 2 bodies call the global
///   \c operator \c new for both.
///
/// \par AI update modules
/// AnimalAIUpdate, GiantBirdAIUpdate, HordeAIUpdate, SiegeAIUpdate,
/// TransportAIUpdate and WanderAIUpdate each have a constructor that starts
/// by running AIUpdateInterface's constructor, and HordeWorkerAIUpdate's
/// starts by running HordeAIUpdate's, so these seven derive from
/// AIUpdateInterface (read from the retail constructors). Their
/// registrations carry the update bit plus the bit Zero Hour uses for the
/// collide interface. AIUpdateInterface's own registration carries the
/// same mask, whereas Zero Hour's AIUpdateInterface registers the update
/// bit alone; this page does not confirm what the extra bit means in
/// BFME 2. FoundationAIUpdate, despite its name, is not
/// in this family (see its class page).
///
/// Every AI module data class here starts from one shared field table, the
/// one TransportAIUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse registers. Read from the
/// retail table, its fields are:
/// - \c Turret, parsed by AIUpdateModuleData::parseTurret (Zero Hour: a
///   nested turret block);
/// - \c AutoAcquireEnemiesWhenIdle, a set of named flags;
/// - durations: \c MoodAttackCheckRate, \c MinCowerTime, \c MaxCowerTime,
///   \c RampageTime, \c BurningDeathTime and
///   \c TimeToEjectPassengersOnRampage;
/// - distances (real numbers): \c StopChaseDistance,
///   \c HoldGroundCloseRangeDistance and \c ComboLocoAttackDistance;
/// - booleans: \c StandGround, \c CanAttackWhileContained,
///   \c RampageRequiresAflame and \c FadeOnPortals;
/// - text: \c AttackPriority and \c AILuaEventsList;
/// - \c ComboLocomotorSet, an index into a list of names, and
///   \c SpecialContactPoints, a list of names that each line appends to.
///
/// Of these, only \c Turret, \c AutoAcquireEnemiesWhenIdle and
/// \c MoodAttackCheckRate are in Zero Hour's AIUpdateModuleData table, which
/// also has \c AltTurret, \c ForbidPlayerCommands and \c TurretsLinked;
/// those three are not in this table.
///
/// \par Special-ability update modules
/// FlingPassengerSpecialAbilityUpdate, ModelConditionSpecialAbilityUpdate,
/// ScaleWallSpecialAbilityUpdate, ToggleDeploySpecialAbilityUpdate,
/// ToggleHiddenSpecialAbilityUpdate, ToggleMountedSpecialAbilityUpdate and
/// WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdate each have a constructor that starts by
/// running the constructor SpecialAbilityUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance
/// runs for a plain SpecialAbilityUpdate, so they derive from
/// SpecialAbilityUpdate (a structural inference from the retail bodies).
/// Each data class's \c buildFieldParse calls
/// SpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse first and then adds a
/// table of its own. The shared table includes \c SpecialPowerTemplate,
/// \c StartAbilityRange, \c PreparationTime, \c PackTime, \c UnpackTime and
/// \c EffectDuration; \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories
/// describes it further. The registrations carry the update interface
/// only, except ToggleMountedSpecialAbilityUpdate's, which adds the bit
/// Zero Hour uses for the create interface; that fits its own
/// \c TriggerInstantlyOnCreate field, but this page does not confirm the
/// bit's BFME 2 meaning. Zero Hour: SpecialAbilityUpdate prepares, packs
/// and unpacks around a special power and fires the effect once the
/// preparation time has passed.
///
/// \par Origins
/// Zero Hour has TransportAIUpdate and WanderAIUpdate, and none of the
/// other thirteen classes. Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has sources for
/// AnimalAIUpdate, FoundationAIUpdate, GiantBirdAIUpdate, HordeAIUpdate,
/// HordeWorkerAIUpdate, SiegeAIUpdate and four of the special-ability
/// updates (ModelCondition, ToggleHidden, ToggleMounted and WeaponFire), so
/// those already exist in BFME 1. FlingPassengerSpecialAbilityUpdate,
/// ScaleWallSpecialAbilityUpdate and ToggleDeploySpecialAbilityUpdate do
/// not appear there. All INI field names on these pages are read from the
/// retail executable's own field tables.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which objects carry these modules, their tags, every
///   field on the class pages and the shared table fields above. The
///   special power that a special-ability update serves is a separate
///   \c SpecialPower definition (\ref page_core_frameworks_upgrades) named
///   by its \c SpecialPowerTemplate field.
/// - Hard-coded: the fifteen module names, their base classes and what
///   each class does with its fields. A new kind of AI or ability needs a
///   new class and a new ModuleFactory::init registration
///   (\ref page_modding_modules).
/// - HordeAIUpdate, HordeWorkerAIUpdate, TransportAIUpdate and
///   SiegeAIUpdate accept only the shared AI fields; there is nothing
///   class-specific to set on their module lines.
/// - Several fields are stored as text when the INI is read (for example
///   \c AttackPriority, \c SpecialWeapon and \c MountedTemplate); this page
///   does not establish when, or whether, a misspelled name is reported.
///   WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdate's constructor is one place where such
///   a name is looked up (see its class page).
/// - Multiplayer: all fifteen run in the game logic, and their data comes
///   from `Object` blocks, so every machine must load identical values
///   (\ref sub_rts_model_modding, \ref page_modding_rules).
/// - Saves: an object saves each module's state as a block keyed by its
///   module tag (\ref sub_objects_modules_lifecycle); renaming a tag in a
///   mod orphans that state in old saves.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The duration fields named on these pages are parsed by
/// INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt, which turns milliseconds into whole logic
/// frames once, when the INI is read
/// (\ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories has the details).
/// AnimalAIUpdate's \c UpdateTimer is read as a plain integer with no such
/// conversion, so its unit is whatever the consuming code expects; that
/// code is not described here. A change to the logic rate
/// (\ref page_frame_loop) therefore affects the two kinds of field
/// differently.
///
/// \see \ref sub_objects_modules, \ref sub_ai_pathfinding,
///      \ref sub_rts_model_flow


/// \class AnimalAIUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories
/// \brief AI update registered as "AnimalAIUpdate", with its own flee and
///        wander settings.
///
/// Its constructor runs AIUpdateInterface's constructor first, so it is an
/// AI update. Its data class adds flee and wander fields to the
/// shared AI fields (AnimalAIUpdateModuleData). What the module does with
/// them is not described here.
///
/// \see AnimalAIUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      AnimalAIUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class AnimalAIUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories
/// \brief Module data for AnimalAIUpdate: the shared AI fields plus flee
///        and wander settings.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// AnimalAIUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template. Its constructor runs the
/// TransportAIUpdateModuleData constructor and its \c buildFieldParse
/// registers TransportAIUpdateModuleData's table before its own.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields, read from the retail table, with the defaults
/// its constructor sets: \c FleeRange (20), \c FleeDistance
/// (100), \c WanderPercentage (50), \c MaxWanderDistance (10),
/// \c MaxWanderRadius (10) and \c UpdateTimer (10), all whole numbers, and
/// \c AfraidOfCastles (true). None of them is converted to logic frames
/// when it is read.

/// \fn Module *AnimalAIUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "AnimalAIUpdate": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's AnimalAIUpdateModuleData for this
///                    module line, shared with every other object of the
///                    template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see AnimalAIUpdate, AnimalAIUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *AnimalAIUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "AnimalAIUpdate": builds the module
///        data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// while an `Object` block is read. It constructs AnimalAIUpdateModuleData
/// with its defaults, then, when \p ini is not null, parses the block with
/// INI::initFromINIMultiProc and AnimalAIUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse,
/// so the block accepts the shared AI fields and the class's own fields.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see AnimalAIUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance, AnimalAIUpdateModuleData


/// \class TransportAIUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories
/// \brief AI update registered as "TransportAIUpdate"; it has no INI
///        fields of its own.
///
/// Its constructor runs AIUpdateInterface's constructor first. The module
/// is registered with the same data factory as AIUpdateInterface itself,
/// TransportAIUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData, so its module line
/// accepts exactly the shared AI fields listed on the group page. Zero
/// Hour: TransportAIUpdate extends the attack commands so that the
/// object's occupants are told to attack as well.
///
/// \see TransportAIUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      TransportAIUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData,
///      TransportAIUpdate::xfer

/// \fn Module *TransportAIUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "TransportAIUpdate": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern; the constructor it runs starts with
/// AIUpdateInterface's.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's shared AI module data for this module
///                    line, built by
///                    TransportAIUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see TransportAIUpdate, TransportAIUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class TransportAIUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories
/// \brief The shared AI module data, under the name the project's ledger
///        gives it: the base that the other AI module data classes here
///        build on.
///
/// Its \c buildFieldParse registers a single table, the shared AI fields
/// listed on the group page, with no base table before it. The data
/// classes of AnimalAIUpdate, GiantBirdAIUpdate, SiegeAIUpdate and
/// WanderAIUpdate register that table first and then their own, and the
/// data factory HordeAIUpdate and HordeWorkerAIUpdate share uses it alone.
///
/// \note The name is not settled by target evidence. The module factory
///       registers this class's data factory for "AIUpdateInterface" as
///       well as for "TransportAIUpdate", the parser of its \c Turret field
///       is named AIUpdateModuleData::parseTurret, and Zero Hour has no
///       TransportAIUpdateModuleData: there TransportAIUpdate uses
///       AIUpdateModuleData. This page treats the class as the shared AI
///       module data.
///
/// \see TransportAIUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *TransportAIUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for both "AIUpdateInterface" and
///        "TransportAIUpdate": builds the shared AI module data for one
///        module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through either
/// registration. It constructs TransportAIUpdateModuleData with its
/// defaults, then, when \p ini is not null, parses the block with
/// INI::initFromINIMultiProc and TransportAIUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse.
/// One function serving two module names is why the AI module lines of
/// both accept the same fields.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see TransportAIUpdate, TransportAIUpdateModuleData


/// \class WanderAIUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories
/// \brief AI update registered as "WanderAIUpdate", with its own wander
///        settings.
///
/// Its constructor runs AIUpdateInterface's constructor first. Unlike
/// Zero Hour, where WanderAIUpdate inherits AIUpdateInterface's data
/// factory and has no fields of its own, BFME 2 registers a data factory of
/// its own, WanderAIUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData. Zero Hour:
/// while the object is idle, WanderAIUpdate's update orders a move to a
/// random nearby point drawn from the logic random generator. This page
/// does not confirm that BFME 2's version behaves the same.
///
/// \see WanderAIUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      WanderAIUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn Module *WanderAIUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "WanderAIUpdate": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern; the constructor it runs starts with
/// AIUpdateInterface's.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's WanderAIUpdateModuleData for this
///                    module line, shared with every other object of the
///                    template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see WanderAIUpdate, WanderAIUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class WanderAIUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories
/// \brief Module data for WanderAIUpdate: the shared AI fields plus four
///        wander settings.
///
/// Its constructor runs the TransportAIUpdateModuleData constructor and its
/// \c buildFieldParse registers TransportAIUpdateModuleData's table before
/// its own.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields, read from the retail table, with the defaults
/// its constructor sets: \c WildBeast (false), \c Selectable
/// (true), \c WanderDistance (30, an unsigned whole number) and
/// \c ConditionForEntry, whose default has every bit set and which is read
/// by a dedicated parser this page does not describe.

/// \fn ModuleData *WanderAIUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "WanderAIUpdate": builds the module
///        data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It constructs WanderAIUpdateModuleData with its defaults, then, when
/// \p ini is not null, parses the block with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and
/// WanderAIUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see WanderAIUpdate, WanderAIUpdateModuleData


/// \class FoundationAIUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories
/// \brief Update module registered as "FoundationAIUpdate"; despite its
///        name it is a plain update module, not an AI update.
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor directly, not
/// AIUpdateInterface's, and then sets the module's wake frame with
/// UpdateModule::setWakeFrame. Its registration carries the update
/// interface alone, and its data factory, which is not documented here,
/// builds a small data class of its own rather than the shared AI data.
/// Read from the retail table, that data accepts two fields:
/// \c RepairHealthPercentPerSecond (a percentage) and \c BuildVariation (a
/// whole number). What the module does with them is not described here.
///
/// \see FoundationAIUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *FoundationAIUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "FoundationAIUpdate": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern. Its body is a few bytes shorter than the
/// others here only because its small allocation size fits a shorter
/// instruction.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see FoundationAIUpdate


/// \class GiantBirdAIUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories
/// \brief AI update registered as "GiantBirdAIUpdate", with settings for
///        attack and return locomotors and for grabbing and tossing.
///
/// Its constructor runs AIUpdateInterface's constructor first. Its data
/// class's \c buildFieldParse registers the shared AI table and then its
/// own.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields, read from the retail table: \c AttackLocomotorType and
/// \c ReturnForAmmoLocomotorType (each an index into a list of names),
/// \c GrabTossTimeTrigger, \c GrabTossHeightTrigger,
/// \c FollowThroughDistance, \c FollowThroughCheckStep and
/// \c FollowThroughGradient (real numbers), and \c TossFX (an FX list,
/// parsed by INI::parseFXList).
///
/// \see GiantBirdAIUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *GiantBirdAIUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "GiantBirdAIUpdate": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see GiantBirdAIUpdate


/// \class HordeAIUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories
/// \brief AI update registered as "HordeAIUpdate"; it has no INI fields of
///        its own.
///
/// Its constructor runs AIUpdateInterface's constructor first. Its data
/// factory, HordeAIUpdate::friend_newModuleData, is shared with
/// HordeWorkerAIUpdate and parses only the shared AI fields. The horde
/// family as a whole, whose core is the HordeContain contain module, is
/// introduced on \ref sub_objects_modules.
///
/// \see HordeAIUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance, HordeWorkerAIUpdate

/// \fn Module *HordeAIUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "HordeAIUpdate": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line,
///                    built by the data factory shared with
///                    HordeWorkerAIUpdate.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see HordeAIUpdate, HordeWorkerAIUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class HordeWorkerAIUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories
/// \brief AI update registered as "HordeWorkerAIUpdate", a specialisation
///        of HordeAIUpdate; it has no INI fields of its own.
///
/// Its constructor runs HordeAIUpdate's constructor first, so it derives
/// from HordeAIUpdate. It is registered with HordeAIUpdate's data factory,
/// so its module line accepts the same fields as HordeAIUpdate's: the
/// shared AI fields. What the worker variant adds is not described here.
///
/// \see HordeWorkerAIUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance, HordeAIUpdate

/// \fn Module *HordeWorkerAIUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "HordeWorkerAIUpdate": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line,
///                    built by HordeAIUpdate's data factory.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see HordeWorkerAIUpdate, HordeAIUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class SiegeAIUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories
/// \brief AI update registered as "SiegeAIUpdate"; it has no INI fields of
///        its own.
///
/// Its constructor runs AIUpdateInterface's constructor first. Its data
/// class has its own \c buildFieldParse, which registers the shared AI
/// table and then a table of its own that is empty in the retail
/// executable, so the module line accepts only the shared AI fields.
///
/// \see SiegeAIUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *SiegeAIUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "SiegeAIUpdate": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see SiegeAIUpdate


/// \class WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories
/// \brief Special-ability update registered as
///        "WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdate": an ability that owns a weapon of
///        its own, named in INI.
///
/// Its constructor runs the SpecialAbilityUpdate constructor first. It
/// then copies the \c SpecialWeapon name from its module data, looks the
/// weapon template up with WeaponStore::findWeaponTemplate and, when the
/// template exists, allocates a weapon for it in the primary weapon slot
/// with WeaponStore::allocateNewWeapon and prepares it for the owning
/// object with Weapon::loadAmmoNow. The module keeps the weapon in a member
/// of its own. How and when it fires is not described here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields (WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData), read from the
/// retail table: \c SpecialWeapon (a weapon name, stored as text),
/// \c WhichSpecialWeapon (a whole number), \c BusyForDuration (a duration)
/// and the booleans \c SkipContinue, \c NeedLivingTargets and
/// \c PlayWeaponPreFireFX. The constructor of the data clears all six.
/// An unknown \c SpecialWeapon name is not reported when the object is
/// built: the module is simply left without a weapon. This page does not
/// describe what the ability does later in that case.
///
/// \see WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories
/// \brief Module data for WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdate: the shared
///        special-ability fields plus the weapon to fire.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData. Its
/// constructor runs SpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData's constructor and its
/// \c buildFieldParse registers SpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData's table
/// before its own. The class page of WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdate lists
/// the own fields.

/// \fn ModuleData *WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdate":
///        builds the module data for one module line and parses its INI
///        block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It constructs WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData with its
/// defaults, then, when \p ini is not null, parses the block with
/// INI::initFromINIMultiProc and
/// WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData

/// \fn Module *WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdate":
///        creates the module, and with it the module's weapon, for one
///        object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The factory itself
/// follows the shared pattern on the group page; the weapon lookup
/// described on the class page happens inside the constructor it runs.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData
///                    for this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdate,
///      WeaponFireSpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData


/// \class FlingPassengerSpecialAbilityUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories
/// \brief Special-ability update registered as
///        "FlingPassengerSpecialAbilityUpdate", with a fling velocity and a
///        landing weapon.
///
/// Its constructor runs the SpecialAbilityUpdate constructor first. The
/// name and fields suggest an ability that throws a passenger and damages
/// where it lands; the code that does so is not described here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields, read from the retail table: \c FlingPassengerVelocity (a
/// 3D vector, parsed by INI::parseCoord3D) and
/// \c FlingPassengerLandingWarhead (a weapon, parsed by
/// INI::parseWeaponTemplate).
///
/// \see FlingPassengerSpecialAbilityUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *FlingPassengerSpecialAbilityUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for
///        "FlingPassengerSpecialAbilityUpdate": creates the module for one
///        object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see FlingPassengerSpecialAbilityUpdate


